const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Scientific and Academic Writing","serviceType":"Academic writing and editing","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/scientific-writing/","description":"Academic manuscripts, reviews, theses and dissertations written and edited to the reporting guideline that governs the design — CONSORT 2025.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What kinds of academic documents do you write and edit?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Research articles and short reports; narrative, scoping and systematic reviews; meta-analyses; case reports to CARE; theses and dissertations at master’s and doctoral level, and the papers that come out of them; conference abstracts and posters; book chapters; and the supporting documents that decide whether any of them are read — cover letters, responses to reviewers, data availability and declaration statements, and completed reporting checklists. Across medicine and the health sciences, the life sciences, engineering and computing, psychology and the social sciences, economics and management, and the humanities. Where a discipline is outside what we can staff properly, we say so and decline rather than assigning a writer who will learn your field on your manuscript."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will editing get my paper accepted?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and the honest version of the answer is more useful than the reassuring one. You will see editing offered as a way to “eliminate rejections due to language, formatting and scientific presentation issues”. In the best-documented study of why reviewers reject — Bordage’s content analysis of 1,053 comments on 151 manuscripts — the leading reasons were inappropriate or incomplete statistics (11.2%), overinterpretation of the results (8.7%), inappropriate instrumentation (7.3%) and a sample too small or biased (5.6%). “Text difficult to follow” came fifth at 3.9%, and writing and presentation as a category ranked ninth of ten. An edit reaches the fifth-ranked problem. What actually improves your odds is fixing the ones above it — which mostly means reporting what you did completely, and claiming only what the interval supports. Note also that no publisher-affiliated editing service claims otherwise: Elsevier’s own guarantee on its editing pages is a re-edit or a refund, not an outcome."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What proportion of papers are desk-rejected?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Nobody knows, and any page giving you a confident percentage is repeating something unsourced. We looked for a publisher-wide figure and there is not one. PLOS, which publishes more editorial data than almost anyone, publishes acceptance rates and decision times by journal and does not publish a desk-rejection rate. What exists is scattered: a peer-reviewed study of 128,454 manuscripts across 25 Nature-branded journals found 34% desk-rejected under single-blind review and 45% under double-blind; Nature itself says only that about 8% of submissions are accepted and that most are declined without review. So the honest range is roughly a third to two-thirds depending on the journal’s selectivity, with no industry figure. We mention this because the top of the search results for this exact question is now largely synthetic content with confident numbers and no sources, and a supplier quoting one of those is telling you how it checks things."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is your turnaround?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Readiness report 3 business days; substantive edit 7; a manuscript written with you 15; a thesis or long-form project 20. Those are the bands, and they are what we will quote against. A turnaround of “less than 8 hours” advertised directly above a package table whose fastest tier is five to seven days cannot be true in both places. Read the table rather than the headline, here and anywhere else. Two things move a real timeline more than word count does: how complete the material is — a manuscript waiting on final analysis outputs is not a writing problem — and your own review cycles, which are almost always the long pole. A paper drafted in fifteen days and reviewed by five co-authors over seven weeks took nine weeks. Expedited delivery is available where capacity allows and is quoted on capacity rather than on urgency; where a date is not achievable we say so rather than taking the fee and missing it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is using a writing service allowed? Do I have to declare it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For journal articles: allowed, and yes, declare it. We found no journal or publisher that prohibits professional editing. Elsevier requires that anyone who participated in substantive aspects such as language editing or medical writing is recognised in the acknowledgements. Springer Nature and BMC require the same and the funding source of that assistance, and warn that unacknowledged editorial services may constitute an undisclosed conflict of interest. The ICMJE is explicit that writing assistance, technical editing, language editing and proofreading do not qualify anyone for authorship — they are acknowledged. So we are named in your acknowledgements, with what we did. For a thesis or any assessed work, the rule is your institution’s, not ours, and institutions differ sharply: some permit language editing within defined limits, some require a declaration, some prohibit third-party assistance entirely. Send us the regulations before you send us the thesis. Where they permit help, we work inside the limits and give you written confirmation of exactly what was done so your declaration is accurate. Where they prohibit it, we decline."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to declare AI use, and where?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Almost certainly yes, and the rule is not the same at every publisher, which is the part that catches people. All of them agree an AI system cannot be an author, because it cannot take responsibility for the work. After that: Elsevier (updated June 2026) wants a separate declaration statement in the manuscript at submission, published with the article, and exempts basic grammar and spelling checking. Wiley (July 2026) requires disclosure of every tool, its purpose, whether it influenced key arguments and how you verified the output — and also exempts tools used solely for spelling and grammar. Springer Nature uses a green/amber/red model rather than one rule, with accountability for content explicitly not delegable to an AI system, and does not mandate where the disclosure goes. Taylor & Francis is the strictest and the one with no stated exemption for grammar tools: full tool name, version number, how it was used and why. The ICMJE’s January 2026 update added a whole new section on AI in publishing. Check your target journal’s instructions to authors rather than assuming a common standard, because there is not one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which reporting guideline applies to my study?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By design, at the versions current on 21 September 2026: randomised trials CONSORT 2025 and their protocols SPIRIT 2025 — both published in April 2025, and both stating that their 2013 and 2010 predecessors should no longer be used. Systematic reviews and meta-analyses PRISMA 2020, which despite its name was published in 2021, with PRISMA-P (2015) for the protocol. Observational studies STROBE (2007), still current — there is no 2025 revision. Diagnostic accuracy STARD 2015. Prediction models TRIPOD+AI (2024), with the 2015 explanation paper still the working document until its successor appears. Animal research ARRIVE 2.0, whose Essential 10 items are the minimum any journal looks for. Economic evaluations CHEERS 2022. Qualitative work COREQ (2007) or SRQR (2014), depending on the journal. Case reports CARE. One caveat we do not skip: the CONSORT extensions, including CONSORT-AI, have not yet been realigned to the 2025 parent, and the 2025 statement tells you to keep using the existing extension in the meantime. If your design is not on that list, the EQUATOR Network indexes several hundred more and we will search it for you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"English is not my first language. How much does that actually cost me?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is measurable, and it is smaller than the market selling you editing implies. Amano and colleagues surveyed 908 environmental scientists across eight nationalities (PLOS Biology, 2023). 38.1% of researchers from moderate-proficiency countries and 35.9% from low-proficiency countries reported having had a paper rejected because of English writing, against 14.4% of native speakers — a rate 2.5 to 2.6 times higher. The revision burden is the starker number: 42.5% of non-native speakers are often or always asked to improve their English during revision, against 3.4% of native speakers. Reading a paper took early-career non-native speakers up to 91% longer; around 30% often or always decline to attend English-language conferences because of it. Three things follow. The disadvantage is real and language editing is worth buying if this is you. It accounts for roughly a third of non-native-speaker rejections, not all of them — and native speakers are rejected for language too. And one detail we will not smooth over: in that study the moderate-proficiency group reported a larger time penalty than the low-proficiency group for writing and presentations, which is what the paper says and not what a neat story would want it to say."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who owns the work, and is my unpublished data safe?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You own it — the manuscript, the data, the figures and every artefact we hand over. A non-disclosure agreement is signed before any material moves, and writers and project staff are bound by internal confidentiality agreements as well. Unpublished data, drafts and decision letters are held under the UK GDPR on access-controlled systems within a quality management system certified to ISO 9001:2015, certificate H3CR9TCU20251212INDQM10N, kept only as long as the project needs them and never used to train anything. We do not reuse your text, figures or search strategies on anyone else’s project, and we do not publish your work as a sample: every sample on this site is redacted or composite. Pubrica will never send you somebody else’s actual manuscript, because the same undertaking covers yours."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I see a sample before ordering?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and there is a complete one on this page — a pre-submission readiness report for a randomised trial that had been desk-rejected once, with the field, the intervention, the institution and every figure changed. Open it above and read the whole thing without asking us for anything, including the parts where the finding was that the authors were right and we changed nothing. If you want something closer to your own work, ask and we will build one round a manuscript like yours: same design, same discipline, same reporting guideline. Two honest caveats. Client work is confidential, so any sample is redacted or composite. And a sample shows method, citation discipline and how findings are reported back to you; it cannot show whether the writer understood your study, which is what the free scope check is for."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if I am not satisfied?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We revise until the manuscript meets the brief and the guideline it is written to, with no cap on rounds inside the scope agreed. Two things keep that workable rather than open-ended. The structure is agreed against the reporting guideline before any prose is written, so a revision is a revision rather than a restart — most dissatisfaction with a manuscript traces back to a structure nobody signed off. And scope is written down: a new analysis, a different journal’s complete reformatting, or a second paper from the same data is new work, and we say which side of that line something falls on before starting rather than after. Where the problem is ours — a guideline version wrong, a number mistranscribed from a table, a reference not opened — correcting it is free and you hear about it from us first. One thing we will not do on request: put back a claim we narrowed. If you want it as you wrote it, it is your paper and your name, and we will restore it and record in writing that we advised against it."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/scientific-writing/"}]}];

